## Problem
`QuarryBlockBreaker.insertIntoSlot` mutates the target inventory without calling `inv.setChanged()`. The empty-slot path uses `inv.setItem(slot, ...)` (which calls `setChanged()` in vanilla `SimpleContainer`, but not necessarily in every `Container` implementation), and the merge path uses `existing.grow(...)` which mutates the `ItemStack` in place and bypasses `setItem` entirely.

Likely consequences:
- Chunk may not be marked dirty → mutated contents may not persist across save/load until another change re-dirties the chunk.
- Open menus viewing the container may not see the update immediately.

## Location
`common/src/main/java/com/logistics/automation/laserquarry/entity/QuarryBlockBreaker.java:109-124`

```java
public static ItemStack insertIntoSlot(Container inv, int slot, ItemStack stack) {
    ItemStack existing = inv.getItem(slot);

    if (existing.isEmpty()) {
        int maxInsert = Math.min(stack.getCount(), Math.min(inv.getMaxStackSize(), stack.getMaxStackSize()));
        inv.setItem(slot, stack.split(maxInsert));
    } else if (ItemStack.isSameItemSameComponents(existing, stack)) {
        int space = Math.min(inv.getMaxStackSize(), existing.getMaxStackSize()) - existing.getCount();
        if (space > 0) {
            int toInsert = Math.min(space, stack.getCount());
            existing.grow(toInsert);
            stack.shrink(toInsert);
        }
    }

    return stack;
}
```

## Suggested fix
Call `inv.setChanged()` after any mutation (both branches). Add a regression test against a `ChestBlockEntity`-style fake (or real `ChestBlockEntity` in a gametest) to verify the chunk is dirtied and a save/reload preserves the merged stack.

